Leave the higher bands to the reference

The higher bands have no linear filter, so the improved filters take no echo away
there. Yet they took the improved suppressor's gain whenever its error in the low
bands was well below the reference's, or about the same and its gain the larger.
In music that gain is often far larger (its lower residual echo estimate takes the
nearend for dominant), and the echo above 8 kHz passed at that gain. They now take
the reference's gain and comfort noise, as AEC3 has them.

Measured in an AEC3-only simulation at 32 kHz (two bands; 40 runs per case, music
and speech, echo alone and with nearend speech): before, music's echo above 8 kHz
was more than 3 dB louder than before the improved filters in 48 of 3,040
windows, up to 28.9 dB; now the higher bands are the same as AEC3's to the sample.
The low bands do not change, nor does anything at 16 kHz.
